<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · Love song · 1939 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">We'll Meet Again</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A British song of 1939 about saying goodbye and hoping to meet again, sung by Vera Lynn to soldiers and their families in the war.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Ross Parker and Hughie Charles</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Ross Parker and Hughie Charles</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Britain in the Second World War: servicemen and their families</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>England</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1939; first recorded 28 September 1939</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march · Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words and music by Ross Parker and Hughie Charles, 1939)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;We'll Meet Again&quot; was written in 1939 by the English songwriters Ross Parker and Hughie Charles and published by Michael Ross Limited, whose directors included Parker; the pianist Norman Keen collaborated with them on it. Vera Lynn first recorded it on 28 September 1939, accompanied by Arthur Young on the Hammond Novachord, an early electronic keyboard; the song is credited as one of the first to use that instrument, the first polyphonic synthesizer. It became one of the best-known songs of the war, speaking to servicemen going off to fight and to the families they left. It gave its name to a 1943 film in which Lynn starred in a loose version of her own life as the &quot;forces' sweetheart&quot;.</p><p>Lynn recorded it again in 1953, with fuller accompaniment and a chorus of British armed forces personnel. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> her recording was among the music held in the twenty underground radio stations of the BBC's Wartime Broadcasting Service, which were to broadcast public information and keep up morale for a hundred days after a nuclear attack. Stanley Kubrick set the 1953 recording to the final scene of Dr. Strangelove (1964), where it accompanies a nuclear war that ends humanity.</p><p>The song kept its place in the remembrance of the war. Lynn sang it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on the 60th anniversary of VE Day in 2005, with Petula Clark and Bruce Forsyth, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> it traditionally closes the Liberation Day Concert in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> on 5 May. On 5 April 2020, in a televised address during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/covid-19-pandemic">COVID-19 pandemic</a>, Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a> echoed it, and Lynn's recording and a charity duet with Katherine Jenkins returned to the British charts that spring, with proceeds for National Health Service charities.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of parting in wartime that holds on to the hope of reunion; tender, calm and consoling.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so the Institute does not print them itself: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="12526916" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/Ross-c-parker-hugh-charles-katherine-jenkins-anthony-ingliss-well-meet-again-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a>.</p></div></section>
</div>
